# Scene
trueqbit with Claude (Opus 5.5), 2026-11-21: step 1 of the config macro
review on chore/config-macro-consistency.

# Decided
- Tiers, one header each: language (toolchain/language_features.h, now
  with the CLASSTYPE_TEMPLATE_ARGS override), stdlib
  (toolchain/stdlib_features.h), platform and compiler identity
  (toolchain/platform.h, new toolchain/compiler.h), SQLite features
  (sqlite3/sqlite3_config.h), capabilities (config/capabilities.h),
  internal (toolchain/compiler_quirks.h, internal_macros.h).
- trueqbit: the quirks are internal although the public header includes
  them, as namespace `internal` is: subject to change without notice.
  They get the SQLITE_ORM_INTERNAL_ prefix.
- trueqbit: SQLITE_ORM_EXPORT keeps its name (as MSVC's STL and others).
- trueqbit: the keyword macros of internal_macros.h keep their short names;
  a `_KW_` infix was mentioned as another option.
- Naming: language features SQLITE_ORM_CPP_<F>_SUPPORTED; library features
  SQLITE_ORM_CPPxx_LIB_<F>_SUPPORTED (trueqbit: the standard version a
  library feature came with is worth carrying).
- Compiler macros keep their names: <maker>_<compiler it presents as>
  (MS_MSVC, GNU_GCC, CLANG_MSVC); clang defines __GNUC__ too, which is why
  GNU_GCC says GNU (trueqbit). The scheme gets stated in compiler.h.

# Discussed
- `-E -dM` of the published sqlite_orm_config.h, g++-16 and clang++ at
  C++17/20/23/26 with a stub sqlite3.h: identical SQLITE_ORM_* sets before
  and after.
- Rejected: HAS_ for language features (Boost reads BOOST_HAS_* as
  non-standard extensions; __has_* are function-like queries).
